# Clean Code Rules

AI-generated code must be **easy to read and maintain**, not clever.

## General

- One file, one primary responsibility
- Functions do one thing; keep them short
- Names reveal intent (avoid abbreviations unless domain-standard)
- No magic numbers — use named constants
- Avoid deep nesting (prefer early returns)
- Do not over-abstract; no one-off helpers
- Reference design IDs in comments, test names, or commit messages (REQ-, API-, SCR-, CMP-, TST-)

## Java (Spring Boot)

- **Controller**: HTTP only — no business logic
- **Service**: business rules and orchestration — `@Transactional` here
- **Repository**: data access only
- **DTO**: request/response shapes — no entity exposure in API
- Package by feature: `feature.<feature-id>.controller|service|repository|dto|entity`
- Exceptions map to HTTP status per design doc error tables

## React (TypeScript)

- Split by `components.md` units — one component file per CMP-ID when practical
- Props interfaces named after component; keep props minimal
- Colocate styles only if project convention allows; otherwise follow existing pattern
- Screen components compose feature components; pages handle routing only
- Loading / error / empty states match `screens.md` state tables

## Tests

- Frontend: Playwright only — file names include TST-ID or CMP-ID
- Backend: JUnit — test method names describe scenario in plain language
- Each TST-ID in `tests.md` maps to at least one automated test

## Do not

- Copy design doc prose into code comments
- Edit `docs/design/` to match implementation
- Add frameworks or patterns not implied by design or existing codebase
